// 26173 RECEIVE BAND CENSUS (the look-down receive fault).
// Symptom: with the Zeus camera above the box, at STEEP look-down, the world
// shadow received ONTO the box flickers into the wrong position while the
// camera moves, and settles the instant it stops.
// RULED OUT FIRST, so nobody re-derives it: this is NOT a view mismatch
// between the mesh transform and the seal pairing. viewAdoptPreframe 1997
// tracks pubLateFrames 1967 one for one, so the mesh IS drawn with the latch
// on 38% of cycles - but ShadowBandFactor consumes i.wpos, and the vertex
// shader is explicit that "wpos and every shading consumer stay ABSOLUTE and
// byte-identical"; only o.pos takes the rebase. The band lookup is therefore
// independent of whatever camera the mesh was rasterised with. Dead end.
// WHAT IS LEFT is the band SELECTION. The shader walks 8 slots finest-first
// and the FIRST slot whose view-z straddles the point and whose uv/z land in
// bounds wins. Each slot is an independently sealed (vcol, sm, atlas copy)
// triple, and they re-seal at very different rates - band0Copies 1107 and
// band1Copies 1108 against 5143 cycles, i.e. ~21%. A slot is internally
// coherent, so it gives the right answer for the epoch it was sealed in; but
// the engine anchors its cascades to the CAMERA, so as the camera moves a
// world point drifts toward the edge of a sealed extent, fails the uv bounds,
// and the walk falls through to a coarser slot with a different epoch and a
// different resolution. That is a JUMP, it is driven by camera motion, it
// stops dead when motion stops, and steep look-down maximises how much of the
// box straddles a boundary. It fits every part of the report.
// IT IS A HYPOTHESIS AND IT GETS MEASURED, NOT BUILT ON. This census runs the
// shader's own selection rule on the CPU, once a cycle, for the caster centre
// (g_sun_map_bounds - the box), and reports which slot wins, how old its seal
// was, and how often the winner changes.
//   bandPickChanges large and tracking the motion -> confirmed; the repair is
//       at the selection (hysteresis on the winner, or re-sealing on extent
//       exit rather than on content change).
//   bandPickChanges ~0 -> the walk is stable and the fault is INSIDE one
//       slot; read bandPickAgeMaxMs, because then it is seal staleness.
//   bandPickNone climbing -> the point leaves every sealed extent and the
//       receive falls off entirely, which is a third thing again.
static uint32_t g_band_pick = 0;            // winning slot for the caster centre (0 = none)
static uint32_t g_band_pick_prev = 0;
static uint64_t g_band_pick_changes = 0;    // winner flips between cycles
static uint64_t g_band_pick_none = 0;       // cycles no sealed slot contained it
// 26175: bandPickChanges was BLIND to the thing it needed to see. It compares
// pick to pick and only remembers a nonzero winner, so the sequence
//     band 1  ->  NONE  ->  band 1
// registered as no change at all. That sequence is a one-cycle receive
// DROPOUT, and a dropout is exactly what a one-frame flicker looks like.
// dump278 on the correct path: bandPickChanges 0 of 1634 picking cycles - the
// walk never flips, that hypothesis is dead - but bandPickNone 417 of 2051,
// one cycle in five with the box centre inside NO sealed band, and
// bandPickAgeMaxMs 148.56 against a typical 30.6.
// 417 scattered cycles would be a permanent shimmer, not a dozen discrete
// events. 417 cycles in ~a dozen contiguous RUNS is the operator's report
// exactly. These two counters tell those apart, and matching the field count
// is the standard of proof this campaign has run on since firePaintPath.
//   noneRuns ~= the flicker count you see -> the receive dropout IS the fault.
//       Fix at whatever drops the box out: the pack-loop skips (twin dedupe,
//       provisional, insane), or the uv/z bounds, or the seal cadence.
//   noneRuns >> what you see -> the dropouts are invisible and the fault is
//       elsewhere; go at the 148 ms seal age instead.
static uint64_t g_band_pick_none_runs = 0;  // entries into a no-band run
// 26176 PROVISIONAL VCOL AT CONSUMPTION - the last candidate standing, and
// the operator's correction is what named it: the receive does not go ABSENT,
// it goes OFFSET. That kills both earlier readings outright.
//     bandPickChanges   0 of 1634   the band walk never flips
//     bandPickNoneRuns  1 against ~5 flickers seen   not dropouts either
// A wrong POSITION in the RIGHT band means the winning slot's own triple is
// internally incoherent. At capture, sm / border / the atlas copy are written
// together and are coherent. vcol is NOT:
//     const bool fv = frame_view_valid && now - frame_view_time < 0.05f;
//     b.vcol[..] = fv ? g_ls.frame_view[..] : g_ro.cycle_pv.view[..];
