// ==========================================================================
// 26249 THE VOLUME TRANSPORT. READ THE STENCIL WHERE THE ENGINE LEFT IT.
//
// WHAT THIS REPLACES AND WHY IT IS A REDESIGN, NOT A PATCH. The verdict this
// feature wants is the stencil COUNT the engine's counting draws left in the
// volume buffer at our footprint. Every build from 26215 to 26248 obtained that
// count LAUNDERED THROUGH THE ENGINE'S SCREEN-SPACE MASK: pre and post bracket
// the stencil resolve, and post/pre isolates the term. That works, and it forced
// two whole mechanisms to exist - the priming pass, because `post` is the mask
// AFTER the engine's resolve and the only way to control our footprint's
// pre-resolve value is to write the engine's own buffer; and the 254/255
// sentinel, because once you are writing the engine's mask you need a way to
// tell your own texels apart from lit background.
//
// THE PRIMING PASS IS THE CASCADE CUT'S CAUSE, DIRECTLY OBSERVED. Mask pixel
// history at a pixel where the box is not visible, one frame, 26240:
//   EID 14608  1.00000 -> 1.00000   engine clears the mask
//   EID 14623  1.00000 -> 0.00000   cascade resolve SHADOWS it, correctly
//   EID 24242  0.00000 -> 1.00000   OUR PRIMING PASS. World shadow gone.
// while the volume-depth history at the SAME pixel shows our injection passing
// (0.982834 -> 0.980128). Our footprint covers a pixel the visible mesh does
// not, because the occlusion guard discarded the mesh there and neither the
// injection (null PS) nor PSMaskPrime honours it. That is the operator's "box
// ghost cutting into other shadows", and it is the 26231 handoff's own ONE
// UNTESTED CASE, verbatim, shipped to default at 26233 without the check.
//
// Save-and-restore was designed and REJECTED: it lets the corruption happen and
// then tries to undo it, and it cannot tell which pixels it is allowed to undo,
// because the resolve legitimately writes inside our footprint too. The
// operator's instruction is explicit - properly, not a workaround. So: read the
// stencil ourselves, and the priming pass has no reason to exist.
//
// WHY IT IS FEASIBLE, AND WHICH PART WAS UNMEASURED. svInjFmt 45
// (D24_UNORM_S8_UINT) with svInjBind 64 (DEPTH_STENCIL only, no
// SHADER_RESOURCE) IS the 26207 class-0 discriminator, and it is why no SRV can
// be made on the volume buffer directly. CopyResource into our own
// R24G8_TYPELESS texture is legal because D24_UNORM_S8_UINT is in that typeless
// family, and the pattern already exists one subsystem over: ensure_sun_depth
// creates a typeless depth with both bind flags, ensure_depth_srv already maps
// R24G8_TYPELESS -> R24_UNORM_X8_TYPELESS, and X24_TYPELESS_G8_UINT is already
// known to this file. THE SAMPLE COUNT WAS THE UNVERIFIED PART. The 26248
// handoff cited shadowTex3Samp 1 as evidence the volume buffer is
// single-sampled; that lane belongs to the 26202 SHADOW DEPTH-TARGET census and
// is a claim about a DIFFERENT resource. The volume buffer's own
// SampleDesc.Count sits in khv_td at the selection stamp and was thrown away.
// svVolSamples now reads it from the latched texture's own desc, MSAA is
// refused outright with its own lane rather than producing an illegal copy, and
// the shape assumptions (ArraySize, MipLevels) are refused the same way.
//
// THE FOOTPRINT WITNESS IS THE ELEGANT PART, AND IT REPLACES A PROXY WITH A
// FACT. The old guard asked "is pre the sentinel?", which is a question about a
// pass we ran. The copy carries the DEPTH plane of the same buffer, so we can
// ask the exact question instead: reconstruct the window z the INJECTION would
// have written for this surface point - lo + ndc * (hi - lo), with the
// injection's own cached viewport range, which is why that range is published
// to the CB here - and compare it against the copied depth at the same texel.
// Within tolerance means the texel IS our surface. That needs no priming, it
// subsumes the disocclusion case rather than approximating it, and it does not
// care whether the background behind us was lit or shadowed - which is the
// honest limit the 26248 sentinel could never get past.
//
// WHAT THE TRANSPORT DOES NOT FIX, STATED SO NOBODY RE-DERIVES IT. The band
// (svReprojPxMean 31-43 px) between the composite view and the injection view
// is untouched. The injection MUST stay in engine space because the volumes
// live there, so the verdict's spatial frame is engine space unavoidably, and
// the mesh must still reach it by reprojection - mode 107 proved the visible
// mesh cannot be moved instead. Reprojection and the epoch ring both survive.
// What dies is the mask being the transport, and with it the cascade cut.
//
// 26249 SHIPS BOTH TRANSPORTS AND DELETES NOTHING. Mode 109 selects this one
// and disables the priming pass, which is what makes the A/B mean something:
// old transport = cascade cut present, new transport = cascade cut absent, one
// variable, one session. The default is unchanged.
//
// g_svs_vol_tex JOINS kh_svs_mask_release - the 26215 lesson, which cost a
// narrow window in which an AddRef'd texture held across a device reset kept a
// dead device alive and left the next boundary creating a view on the OLD
// device. So does the latched source, on the same terms as g_svs_mask_cand.
// ==========================================================================
static ID3D11Texture2D*          g_svs_vol_src = nullptr;   // AddRef'd volume buffer
static void*                     g_svs_vol_src_id = nullptr;   // weak identity
static ID3D11Texture2D*          g_svs_vol_tex = nullptr;   // our typeless copy
static ID3D11ShaderResourceView* g_svs_vol_depth_srv = nullptr;   // t23
static ID3D11ShaderResourceView* g_svs_vol_sten_srv = nullptr;    // t24
