// ==========================================================================
// 26257 THE ENGINE-VIEW LOCATOR. STAGE ONE OF TWO, AND IT DELIBERATELY CHANGES
// NOTHING.
//
// WHERE THE CAMPAIGN IS. Three measurements, all byte-level:
//   the engine uses ONE view per frame (volume pass == colour pass, 0.0000 px)
//   our COMPOSITE equals it exactly (0.0 px)
//   our INJECTION is 21.5 deg / 721 px away in the same frame
// and the two channels available at seam time are BOTH wrong:
//   frame_view sampled early   svReprojPxMean   94.9 px
//   cycle_pv (the bridge)      injCycleErrMean 117.6 px
// with injCycleIncoherent 0 and the arms agreeing on frame counts, so those two
// numbers are trustworthy. The operator's mode-102 test said the same thing
// visually before the gauge did. There is no third CPU-side source: the value
// exists in ENGINE memory before its first draw (EID 47 already uses it) and we
// only learn it through the cascade-upload stream, which is 589 writes a frame
// and alternates between this frame's camera and the next.
//
// SO THE VIEW COMES OUT OF THE ENGINE'S OWN CONSTANT BUFFER. Stage two will
// CopySubresourceRegion it GPU-side at the seam and let the injection's vertex
// shader build its transform from it - same frame, no readback, no stall. That
// needs a BYTE OFFSET, and a wrong offset would rasterize our footprint to an
// arbitrary transform: an artifact far worse than the lag AND indistinguishable
// from "the whole approach is wrong". So the offset is located and VALIDATED
// first, and stage two is built on a number rather than on a hope.
//
// THE SEARCH HAS ONE EXACT CRITERION, NOT A HEURISTIC. RenderDoc measured our
// composite EQUAL to the engine's view, so the 4-row block in the engine's CB
// whose 3x3 matches our composite's basis IS the engine's view block. That is
// self-validating - the best-match residual is the acceptance number - and it
// cannot make the mistake an orthonormality search would: EID 47's b2 rows 0-3
// are an IDENTITY WORLD MATRIX, which is orthonormal, and my python
// _find_basis latched onto exactly that and published a 166 deg nonsense
// verdict. One criterion that can be wrong in a measurable way beats three that
// cannot.
//
// THE OFFSET IS LOCATED ONCE AND REUSED, which is the lightLoc pattern this file
// already runs successfully (lightLocOff 40, lightLocFloats 56, lightLocHits
// 4.0e6, lightLocMisses 0, lightLocRelocs 0). Offsets are a property of the
// shader family and do not move; only the values change. So the SLOW structural
// part (finding where, one frame late through a staging Map with DO_NOT_WAIT -
// the cascbind_step pattern) is separated from the FAST part (reading what,
// same frame, stage two). One frame of latency is fatal for the VALUE and
// harmless for the ADDRESS.
//
// 26264 THAT ACCEPTANCE TEST IS RETIRED, and plainly: it never passed. Its lanes
// measured a candidate block against OUR COMPOSITE's basis, and capture 6
// disproved the premise that the composite equals the engine's view. Lanes and
// reference machinery are deleted; the SELF-CONTAINED fingerprint below replaced
// them. That does not fire yet either, for a reason 26263 measured and
// RENDER_HANDOFF_CAMPAIGN_37.md carries forward: the pipeline state bound when
// the stage runs is not the state the RenderDoc capture shows at that draw.
// ==========================================================================
// 26259 THREE DEFECTS IN THE LOCATOR, ALL MINE, ALL PINNED AGAINST THE CAPTURE.
// injEngLocHits 0 / injEngLocMiss 1471 with injEngStageBusy 32 - the staging and
// the readback worked on every frame and the SEARCH found nothing.
//
// (1) TRANSPOSED. I compared the candidate block's COLUMN 0 against the engine's
//     X AXIS. The engine stores its basis as ROWS: at EID 47's b2, float 16 is
//     (0.735421, 0.000000, 0.677610), which IS the X axis, and our composite's
//     col0 equals it to six decimals. Reading khe_f[o + r*4 + 0] took
//     (0.735421, -0.041144, -0.676360) instead - the column - for an error of
//     1.395 against a 0.05 threshold. Correct indexing gives EXACTLY 0.
// (2) WRONG SLOT. The stage picked "the widest" bound CB, and every engine CB
//     reports ByteWidth 65536, so the tie always resolved to slot 0 -
//     injEngLocSlot 0 in the dump - while the view block sat in b2. All four
//     slots are staged and scanned now, which is what cascbind_step already
//     does and what I should have copied instead of improvising.
// (3) EPOCH KEY OFF BY ONE. The stage stamps g_svs_frame_seq at the BRACKET
//     (= N); the composite stamps it after the seam boundary has bumped it
//     (= N+1). So the scan paired the staged copy against the PREVIOUS frame's
//     composite - a whole frame of rotation, tens of pixels, guaranteed miss.
//     Same one-frame shift svVolAgeMax 1 documents, and 26250 already
//     established the right key for it.
//
// Any one of these alone produces 1471 misses, so the three could only be
// separated by pinning each against known-good capture data rather than by
// re-running. THIS IS WHY THE LOCATOR SHIPPED BEFORE THE GPU COPY: with the copy
// layered on top, all three would have presented as one arbitrary footprint
// transform and there would have been nothing to diff against.
static ID3D11Buffer* g_svs_eng_stage[6] = {};      // staging readback, one per VS slot
static uint32_t g_svs_eng_stage_bytes[6] = {};
// 26265: the byte offset the window was taken FROM, per slot. The fingerprint's
// offset is window-relative; adding this gives the ABSOLUTE address in the
// resource, which is the only address that survives the stage draw and the copy
// draw being different shader families. Ledger at g_svs_eng_blk_abs.
static uint32_t g_svs_eng_stage_first[6] = {};
// 26266: the bound numConstants, recorded rather than derived. The staging window
// is min(range, 1024), so window/16 equals the bound range only while the range
// fits - and a mismatch there would make the copy's binding fingerprint
// false-reject every frame on any slot bound wider than 1 KB. The bug that lane
// exists to catch must not be one the lane manufactures.
static uint32_t g_svs_eng_stage_num[6] = {};
static uint8_t  g_svs_eng_stage_pending = 0;       // bitmask
static ID3D11Buffer* g_svs_eng_stage_src[6] = {};  // 26262: AddRef'd sources of the
                                                   // staged copies, so a fingerprint
                                                   // hit one frame later can latch
                                                   // the resource it came from
static uint32_t g_svs_eng_stage_mask = 0;          // 26260: slots staged last time
static uint32_t g_svs_eng_stage_last_bytes = 0;    // 26260: total bytes staged
// ==========================================================================
// 26262 THE ENGINE-VIEW TRANSPORT. State for the same-frame GPU copy.
//
// THE FINGERPRINT IS SELF-CONTAINED, which is the whole reason this build can
// exist. Every earlier locator matched the candidate block against OUR
// COMPOSITE's basis, which needed an epoch pairing between a staged copy and a
// composite fill one frame apart - and that pairing was wrong twice (26257 keyed
// on g_svs_frame_seq, which the seam boundary bumps in between). Worse, the
// reference was not reliable: capture 6 shows our composite 16 degrees out in
// PITCH from the engine in a fast vertical flick, while that same frame's engine
// volume pass and engine colour pass are byte-identical to each other. So the
// composite is not a fixed point to measure against - it is a second patient.
//
// The engine's view buffer identifies itself instead, with no reference at all:
//     rows 0..2   3x3 == IDENTITY, .w column a plausible camera position
//     rows 4..6   3x3 ORTHONORMAL
//     row  7      (0,0,0,1)
// Three independent structural facts on one buffer. No epoch, no pairing, no
// composite - and therefore none of the three failure modes that cost the last
// three sessions.
//
// LATCH THE RESOURCE, NOT THE SLOT. The same buffer is bound at b2 by the
// volume-pass family and b3 by the colour-pass family, so the slot is a property
// of the shader and the resource is the thing that is stable. Each frame the
// copy finds which of the four bound slots holds the latched identity - four
// pointer compares, no readback - and copies 64 bytes from byte 64. Weak
// identity, same discipline as g_svs_mask_cand, and it dies with the device.
// ==========================================================================
static ID3D11Buffer* g_svs_eng_src = nullptr;      // AddRef'd engine view CB
static void*    g_svs_eng_src_id = nullptr;        // weak identity
